Default Constructors - 
	A default constructor is a no-argument constructor automatically provided by the Java compiler when a class does not explicitly define any constructor. It initializes instance variables to their default values and implicitly calls the superclass's no-argument constructor.
	
String  → null
int     → 0
boolean → false
double  → 0.0

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Parameterized Constructors - 
	A parameterized constructor is a constructor that accepts one or more parameters and is used to initialize an object's instance variables with specific values when the object is created

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	
Copy Constructor - 
	A copy constructor is a constructor that creates a new object by copying the state of an existing object of the same class.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	
Constructor chaining - 
	Constructor chaining is the process of calling one constructor from another constructor to reuse initialization logic and avoid duplicate code.
	
Java provides two ways:
	this() → calls another constructor in the same class
	super() → calls a constructor of the parent class
	
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Key words & There Concepts - 

this - 
	this is a reference variable that refers to the current object. It is used to access the current object's instance variables and methods, call another constructor in the same class using this(), and pass or return the current object.
	
super - 
	super is a reference keyword used to refer to the immediate parent class. It is used to access the parent class's variables and methods and to call the parent class's constructor.
